🔵At 34, #PaulKagame Led #Rwanda’s Liberation. Today, His Leadership Defines a Nation’s Transformation 

Ugandan businessman and public commentator @FrankGashumba has offered a compelling account of President #PaulKagame’s role in Rwanda’s liberation, highlighting his leadership at just 34 years old and the transformation of a country emerging from the devastation of the 1994 Genocide against the Tutsi.

Speaking during an interview with a journalist, Gashumba recounted the history of the Rwandan liberation struggle, beginning with the plight of Rwandan refugees in #Uganda and their determination to return to their homeland.

He recalled the roles played by the late Major General Fred Rwigyema and President Kagame in the struggle to end exile and restore Rwandans’ right to return home.

Following Rwigyema’s death at the beginning of the liberation struggle in October 1990, Kagame returned from military training in the United States and assumed leadership of the Rwanda Patriotic Army (RPA), the armed wing of the Rwandan Patriotic Front.

Gashumba particularly emphasised #Kagame’s age when he assumed that responsibility.

“Do you know how old he was? 34 years,” he remarked, drawing attention to the responsibility Kagame assumed in leading the struggle that culminated in the RPA’s victory in July 1994.

But Gashumba’s account went beyond military history. He pointed to the enormous challenge of rebuilding Rwanda after the 1994 Genocide against the Tutsi, when the country’s infrastructure and institutions had suffered widespread destruction.

He highlighted the transformation of Kigali, particularly its nighttime appearance, clean roads, street lighting and organised urban environment.

“When you land in Kigali at night, you might think you’ve landed in a small town in Europe. The lights, clean roads, everything is in order,” Gashumba observed.

His central message was that President Kagame’s historical role and the scale of Rwanda’s subsequent reconstruction should not be dismissed or underestimated.

Gashumba’s remarks offer a perspective on Rwanda’s journey from liberation to reconstruction, placing particular emphasis on the young leadership that emerged during one of the most consequential periods in the country’s history.

